# HbTK TODO

## Purpose

HbTK is the complete visual toolkit for Hackedbox.

It is not intended to be merely a collection of widgets. HbTK provides the rendering, widgets, visual styling, theming, and eventually the user-facing tools used to create and manage Hackedbox styles.

The existing Blackbox/Hackedbox visual system is the foundation and compatibility baseline. HbTK expands that system beyond the traditional limitations of Blackbox themes.

The goal is to allow users to retain the classic Hackedbox character while having the freedom to create substantially more modern visual designs.

---

# Version 1 Requirements

## 1. Style Engine

Build a centralized HbTK StyleEngine responsible for the visual appearance of HbTK components.

The StyleEngine should provide a common styling system for:

* Windows
* Dialogs
* Menus
* Buttons
* Entries
* Containers
* Future widgets
* Hackedbox visual components that migrate into HbTK

Styling should not be hard-coded independently inside individual widgets.

### Style properties

The StyleEngine should eventually support:

* Colors
* Fonts
* Font sizes
* Text rendering
* Borders
* Border widths
* Corner radius
* Backgrounds
* Transparency
* Opacity
* Gradients
* Shadows
* Padding
* Margins
* Widget dimensions
* Spacing
* Titlebar appearance
* Menu appearance
* Widget states
* Icons
* Visual effects

---

# 2. Modern Rendering Capabilities

HbTK must extend beyond the traditional Blackbox rendering model.

The engine should support visual features such as:

* Rounded corners
* Curved UI elements
* Transparent elements
* Semi-transparent elements
* Layered backgrounds
* Gradients
* Shadows
* Custom borders
* Custom titlebar shapes
* Per-widget visual effects
* Modern typography
* Icon-based controls

These features should be available to the StyleEngine rather than implemented as one-off features in individual applications.

---

# 3. Classic Hackedbox Compatibility

The new system must not discard the existing Hackedbox visual identity.

The default HbTK style should be capable of reproducing the traditional Hackedbox/Blackbox appearance.

Classic styling therefore becomes one style among many rather than the maximum capability of the system.

Conceptually:

```
Classic Hackedbox style
         +
   HbTK rendering
         +
   StyleEngine
         =
Extended Hackedbox styling
```

---

# 4. Widget Styling

All HbTK widgets should obtain their appearance through the StyleEngine.

Initial widgets include:

* Window
* Dialog
* Widget
* WidgetContainer
* Entry
* Button

Each widget should support appropriate visual states.

### Example: Entry

* Normal
* Focused
* Disabled
* Read-only
* Placeholder text
* Cursor
* Selection

### Example: Button

* Normal
* Hover
* Pressed
* Disabled
* Focused

The widget should provide behavior and geometry while the StyleEngine determines how that state is rendered.

---

# 5. Painter / Rendering Layer

Expand the existing `Painter` into the rendering foundation used by HbTK.

Current responsibilities include:

* Filling rectangles
* Drawing borders
* Drawing text
* Allocating colors

Future responsibilities may include:

* Rounded rectangles
* Alpha compositing
* Transparency
* Gradients
* Shadows
* Clipping
* Images
* Icons
* Better font handling
* Coordinate translation
* Layered rendering

The rendering layer should remain lightweight and appropriate for Hackedbox.

---

# 6. Widget Geometry and Layout

Complete the HbTK coordinate and layout model.

The current widget hierarchy establishes parents but does not yet provide a complete coordinate transformation system.

The toolkit needs consistent handling of:

* Parent-relative coordinates
* Child coordinates
* Window coordinates
* Nested containers
* Resizing
* Minimum/maximum sizes
* Padding
* Margins
* Spacing
* Basic layout management

Widgets should render at their assigned geometry without requiring individual widgets to know about the top-level X window.

---

# 7. Input and Interaction

Complete the HbTK event system.

Required areas include:

* Mouse press
* Mouse release
* Mouse movement
* Keyboard press
* Keyboard release
* Focus handling
* Widget activation
* Keyboard navigation
* Enter/Return activation
* Escape/cancel behavior
* Tab navigation
* Modifier handling

Buttons need an actual activation/callback mechanism.

Entries need real text input and cursor editing.

---

# 8. Runbox

Use Runbox as one of the first complete HbTK applications.

Runbox should demonstrate the StyleEngine rather than contain its own permanent visual styling.

Planned Runbox functionality:

* Command entry
* Command history
* History/dropdown control
* Run
* Cancel
* Keyboard operation
* Enter to execute
* Escape to cancel
* Focus handling

The visual design should demonstrate that classic Hackedbox styling and newer HbTK styling can coexist.

Runbox should not become a collection of hard-coded colors and drawing operations.

---

# 9. Style Definition / Theme Format

Create a dedicated format for HbTK styles.

The format should describe appearance rather than executable behavior.

It should eventually support:

* Global style properties
* Window properties
* Menu properties
* Widget properties
* State-specific properties
* Fonts
* Colors
* Transparency
* Geometry
* Borders
* Radius
* Gradients
* Icons
* Effects

The format should be designed so styles can be created and edited without recompiling Hackedbox.

---

# 10. Style Manager

Create the HbTK component responsible for:

* Loading styles
* Validating styles
* Applying styles
* Switching styles
* Reloading styles
* Saving style information
* Managing the active style

Style changes should be capable of propagating to visible HbTK components.

---

# 11. Style Builder / Front End

Create a user-facing frontend for building HbTK styles.

The long-term goal is that users can spend their time **designing styles rather than manually editing theme files**.

The style builder should eventually provide visual controls for:

* Colors
* Fonts
* Borders
* Corner radius
* Transparency
* Opacity
* Gradients
* Shadows
* Widget dimensions
* Spacing
* Titlebars
* Menus
* Buttons
* Entries
* Other HbTK widgets

It should provide a live preview where practical.

The user should be able to:

1. Create a style.
2. Modify visual properties.
3. Preview the result.
4. Save the style.
5. Load it into Hackedbox.
6. Continue editing later.

The frontend is part of the overall HbTK vision, not an unrelated desktop configuration utility.

---

# 12. Icons and Visual Assets

Provide a common HbTK mechanism for:

* Icons
* Images
* Background assets
* Widget icons
* Menu icons
* Dropdown indicators
* Other visual assets

Styles should be able to reference these assets without requiring application-specific implementations.

---

# 13. Hackedbox Integration

Gradually move Hackedbox visual functionality onto HbTK.

The long-term boundary should be:

```
Hackedbox
    = window manager / desktop behavior

HbTK
    = visual system / UI toolkit / theming
```

Hackedbox should request visual components from HbTK rather than independently implementing duplicate visual systems.

Areas for eventual migration include:

* Menus
* Configuration UI
* Runbox
* Dialogs
* Future desktop utilities
* Other user-facing visual components

---

# 14. Architecture Goals

HbTK should remain:

* Lightweight
* X-based
* Independent of GTK
* Independent of Qt
* Independent of a full desktop environment
* Suitable for Hackedbox
* Reusable by other Hackedbox utilities
* Extensible without rewriting existing widgets

The toolkit should separate:

```
Behavior
    ↓
Widget
    ↓
Style
    ↓
Rendering
```

A widget should describe what it is and what state it is in.

The StyleEngine should determine how that state looks.

The Painter/rendering layer should determine how that appearance is drawn.

